  integration:
    count: 9
    covers: >-
      ui/src/App.test.tsx (7 new, 34 total) over the composed screen against a mock API - this WU's bounded
      contract is the whole flow-list surface, so every assertion is made through the real header beside the
      real filters rather than against a component in isolation. The acts sit LAST among the header's
      controls (asserted by index, so a control added before them cannot silently move them off the trailing
      edge) and take no room until asked for. Export is scoped by whatever the filters selected, driven by
      choosing a host from the real dropdown and asserting the request carries it. Clearing empties the list
      into the absence only a clear produces and the count follows - and the rows move only when the bus
      fires, because the mock's clearFlows answers a count and nothing else, which is exactly the dependency
      the screen has on the real system. The cleared absence gives way when rows return, and an emptying
      that is NOT a clear lands on "no flows yet" - the flag can only ever describe the emptiness it caused.
      Narrowing after a clear reads as the filter's absence. An imported file lands as marked rows the list
      gets from the bus. A pasted command is refused first (the sentence verbatim, the list untouched, so
      nothing was stored) and then accepted, landing as a request-only marked row.
      tests/api.rs (2 new, 42 total) over the real axum router: a cross-origin client can read the filename
      the export stamped - the preflight AND the real response both carrying the expose grant, because the
      browser checks each in turn; and every door the app calls answering what it did and refusing in a
      sentence - export scoped by the same filter the listing is, that document imported back, a curl
      command imported, an unsupported flag refused with the flag NAMED in the body (which is what the paste
      target renders), a filtered clear reporting what left, and both doors' rows marked. Writing this one
      found the request-only/HAR fact recorded in the result.
  e2e:
    waived: >-
      The app half is the epic's own WebDriver row - launch the built app against the running engine and
      drive export -> clear -> import -> paste - which lands with the epic per its e2e plan, and which is
      also the ONLY thing that can confirm the minWidth change against a real window, which is why design
      run 11 names it there rather than here. A WU-level e2e would build the same app and drive the same
      controls through the same window. Same reasoning W1-W4 and W7 recorded, and the epic gate re-walks it.
      The two existing e2e suites (e2e_capture 6, e2e_h2 4) run green unchanged, which is the regression
      evidence that nothing on the capture path moved.
note: >-
  E-EXPORT.W6 builds the capture-management controls design run 11 specified, and cites that run rather
  than the superseded run 10. Four things carry the WU. First, the build consumes the run's proposals
  without minting any of them: Menu and the modal frame are built local and the ⋮ face rides `children`,
  because Dialog, Menu and the `more` glyph entry all have named owners and the run is explicit that a
  build may use them and must not mint them - so what lands is three proposals now proven in one call site
  each, which is a better starting point for their owners than a description was. ModalShell moving to a
  module of its own is the one judgement call there: its warrant for staying local was a statement about
  the collection ("a general Dialog drawn from the app's only dialog would generalise from a sample of
  one"), not about the file, and a second screen importing it out of a sibling screen is the shape the
  collection exists to prevent. Second, the build pays two things the design could not reach because
  neither is a design artifact: minWidth 800 -> 850, above the 846px at which the header wraps, guarded by
  a test that reads the shipped config; and Access-Control-Expose-Headers on the CORS grant, without which
  Content-Disposition is invisible to a cross-origin reader and the app would have to invent the filename
  the server already stamps - the run listed that filename as a USED capability, so the grant is what makes
  the capability true rather than an extra. Third, the list refills from the bus in every case and never
  from a mutation's own response, which closed a live gap the run had spotted by reading the code: the
  server names its clear event `cleared` and stream() registered request/response/evicted/lagged only, so
  before this WU a clear emptied the store and left the rows on screen. Fourth, the absences. FlowList
  carried a comment saying its two wordings must not share a sentence, because telling someone to send
  traffic they have already sent is the failure the second exists to prevent - and a user who has just
  cleared the capture is exactly that person, which is the build fact that overturned run 10's answer. The
  third absence is tested pairwise-distinct rather than spot-checked, so a future edit that collapses two
  of them is red. One deviation is carried rather than introduced: the epic plan named useConfirm for the
  clear confirmation and both design runs found it a ~2 s after-the-fact flash that reports rather than
  asks, so the ask is a modal and the plan's claim stays wrong on the record. Two smaller calls are worth
  naming because a reviewer would otherwise read them as drift: `import` is disabled while the textarea is
  empty rather than refusing with a sentence, because every refusal wording on this surface is the
  server's and a client-side one would put text on screen that no contract owns; and testId became a
  prefix so two dialogs on one screen have addressable parts, chosen so ProxyCloseDialog keeps all three of
  its existing test ids verbatim and no shipped test moved. Writing the integration test found one fact
  the epic had not stated: har_entry requires a response, so a pasted curl command is invisible to
  GET /flows/export while appearing in GET /flows. That is W2's deliberate contract - a HAR entry without a
  response is not a HAR entry - and it is recorded in the result as a follow-up rather than worked around.
  Suite green, every figure read off a run rather than counted from source: 827 UI tests in 48 files
  (up from 741 in 44 at the WU's start, 86 new across eight suites), 221 lib unit, 41 tests/api.rs, 3 tests/ca.rs,
  6 tests/e2e_capture.rs, 4 tests/e2e_h2.rs, 0 failures; cargo fmt --check and clippy -D warnings clean
  across all targets; pnpm lint (design/vocabulary) clean with one design-allow pragma on the same
  offset-from-the-trigger shape Select's popup already carries.
  A fifth pass ran after the four review rounds and is the reason for the last commit: the built app was
  driven BY HAND, in a browser, against a real core on spare ports - not the wdio suite. It found two
  defects no test here could have caught as written. The listening ref that round 2 added to keep an
  abandoned import from reaching back was armed at its DECLARATION and disarmed in a cleanup, so
  StrictMode's mount/unmount/remount disarmed it permanently and a successful import never closed its own
  ask; the app was unusable for import and 814 green tests said otherwise, because every one of them
  rendered the component unwrapped while main.tsx ships it inside StrictMode. And the import ask's
  textarea is its own opener, so disabling it mid-import blurred focus to <body>, outside the dialog,
  where ModalShell's key handler never sees Esc - the shipped unit test passed only because it fires the
  key AT the dialog node, bypassing focus entirely. Both now have regression tests proven red against the
  old code. The commit also carries three things that are additions rather than fixes: a face on each menu
  act, taken from what the app already spends on that direction (IconButton's save glyph, the very mark
  FlowList prints on an imported row, and one new one for erase); a ring in the three places a transfer is
  visible; and the import ask's words reordered to lead with the command, which is what design run 11's own
  T4 decided ("the paste offers the file, not the reverse") and what the shipped copy contradicted. Spinner
  is build-local rather than a collection entry for ModalShell's stated reason - no registry entry owns it,
  promoting it is the collection's to perform, and the alternative was a third and fourth copy of the six
  classes Select and StatusLine each hand-roll.
review_rounds: >-
  Four rounds of the code-review skill ran against this WU; each round's record is the cr note on the tip
  it judged. Round 1 (five finders, eleven candidates) confirmed three: a T2 doc calling design run 11
  "archived" when archival is an epic-close event; openFile and its browser fallback shipping with no unit
  test; and the export and import sharing one in-flight slot, so dismissing the import ask mid-export
  cleared the export's line. Round 2 re-read the fix and confirmed four more, the first of which says round
  1's fix was half a fix: guarding the slot's CLEAR left its SET unguarded, so an import that actually ran
  mid-export still overwrote the export's mark and the export's finally then cleared it, re-offering an act
  that was still running - the slot is now two independent flags. Also: an abandoned import still called
  onDone() on the mounted parent, closing whichever ask the user had opened since; the export door now
  throws the server's sentence like the other two, a finding whose verifier refuted it on the strength of
  THIS WU's own plan, which does not outrank the design run it cites (run 11's Interaction table spells out
  the export row); and the result's per-suite test count was stale because a test.each block yields more
  cases than it has test( calls. Round 3 found nothing in the code - correctness traced every export/import
  interleaving and both flags and returned empty - and five more wrong numbers in this WU's own record,
  including two in an earlier version of THIS note (a lib count and an api.rs count that were never
  measured). Every count above is now read off a run. That is the WU's one recurring defect class and it is
  the same one E-EXPORT.W9 named: a value moves and a sentence quoting the old value survives. Every
  confirmed code finding carries a regression test written red first; six of the tests counted above exist
  because of these rounds - four in CaptureControls.test.tsx, two in body.test.ts. Round 4 then found three
  more false statements, two of them the same guessed count ("eight" where six is right) in this note and
  in the result, and one a "Two rounds" opener the same document later contradicted. Eight false statements
  across rounds 3 and 4, all one class, none of them in the code. W9's result named this exact class six
  times over one WU earlier and reached a structural answer; it did not hold here, and that - not the
  individual numbers - is what this WU hands the epic.
  A fifth pass was not a review round but a manual end-to-end session: the user asked for the built app to
  be driven by hand rather than by the wdio suite. Seven defects were found and reported; two were fixed in
  the last commit (the StrictMode-disarmed listening ref, and Esc dying while the ask's opener is
  disabled). One candidate was RETRACTED before reporting - the export appearing not to download was
  Browser.setDownloadBehavior resetting per CDP session, not saveBody's immediate revokeObjectURL, proven
  by an A/B against the exact shipped pattern - and one measurement was withdrawn as a timing artifact
  after re-reading it in a single evaluation. Five findings are NOT addressed here and are the epic's or a
  later WU's: a filtered clear still announcing "capture cleared" with rows on screen; the export silently
  dropping response-less flows, which is W2's contract and already recorded in this WU's result; the menu
  trigger lacking aria-haspopup/aria-expanded, which needs IconButton to pass them through; the export
  failure notice taking 106px from the filter field with no dismiss; and the list ordering by insertion
  rather than by the TIME column it displays, which DEC-35 does not cover. The session also verified the
  minWidth 800 -> 850 change against a real window at 800 and 850, which is the one thing this WU said
  only the epic's e2e could confirm.
